//@ compile-fail
//@ error-contains: a procedure with no value needs no

// `null` is a value; `()` is the absence of one. A procedure returns nothing and needs no
// `null` to say so, so a unit-returning body may not end in `null`. The annotated lambda
// `(n) => null` is already rejected against a `-> ()` arrow; this is the same rule on the
// fn-body path, where the tail was the one position the check used to be skipped -- the
// `-> null` mistake wearing a tail. An effectful non-`null` tail (a discarded `g()`) stays
// legal; only `null` is barred.

fn f() -> () {
    null
}

fn main() {
    f();
}
